**DEFINITION 3.1.** The *elements* of the information system $\mathbf{A} = (D_{\mathbf{A}}, \Delta_{\mathbf{A}}, \mathrm{Con}_{\mathbf{A}}, \vdash_{\mathbf{A}})$ are those subsets $x$ of $D_{\mathbf{A}}$ where:

(i) all finite subsets of $x$ are in $\mathrm{Con}_{\mathbf{A}}$; and

(ii) whenever $u \subseteq x$ and $u \vdash_{\mathbf{A}} X$, then $X \in x$.

We write $x \in |\mathbf{A}|$ to mean $x$ is an element of the system. This set of elements is the *domain* determined by the given system. An element that is not included in any strictly larger element in the domain is called a *total element*; the set of total elements is denoted by $\mathrm{Tot}_{\mathbf{A}}$. ■

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`InfoSys.Element`).** An element of an information system is a set of data that is finitely consistent—every finite subset lies in $\mathrm{Con}$—and deductively closed: whenever a finite set of data from the carrier entails a further datum, that datum belongs to the carrier. The domain $|\mathbf{A}|$ is the collection of all such elements.

**Theorem (`InfoSys.Element.ext`).** Two elements are equal precisely when they have the same carrier.

*Proof.* An element is determined by its carrier together with the consistency and closure witnesses, which are propositions. □

**Theorem (`InfoSys.Element.le_refl`).** Carrier inclusion is reflexive on elements.

*Proof.* Every set is a subset of itself. □

**Theorem (`InfoSys.Element.le_trans`).** Carrier inclusion is transitive on elements.

*Proof.* Subset is transitive. □

**Theorem (`InfoSys.Element.le_antisymm`).** Carrier inclusion is antisymmetric on elements.

*Proof.* Mutual inclusion of carriers yields equal carriers, hence equal elements. □

**Definition (`InfoSys.Element.instPartialOrder`).** The domain of elements is partially ordered by inclusion of carriers.
